  "working": "Identify central metal ion oxidation state and geometry from formula. sp³ hybridisation corresponds to tetrahedral or square planar? sp³ is tetrahedral (four ligands). Among given complexes, those with four ligands likely tetrahedral: [CuCl₄]²⁻, [CoCl₄]²⁻, [Zn(NH₃)₄]²⁺, [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻, [PdCl₄]²⁻. Others have six (octahedral) or square planar (Pt complexes) or coordination number 6. So only four-ligand species considered. Determine paramagnetism: need unpaired electrons. Use typical electron configurations and ligand field strength. Approximate: Cu(II) d9 -> one unpaired (paramagnetic). Co(II) d7 high spin tetrahedral -> three unpaired? Co(II) tetrahedral usually high spin d7 gives three unpaired. Zn(II) d10 -> diamagnetic. Ni(II) with CN⁻ strong field -> low spin d8 square planar? But tetrahedral Ni(II) d8 could be two unpaired? However [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ likely square planar? Actually Ni(II) with CN⁻ often forms square planar complex [Ni(CN)4]²⁻ which is diamagnetic (low spin d8). Pd(II) d8 square planar diamagnetic. So paramagnetic among four-ligand: [CuCl₄]²⁻ (paramagnetic), [CoCl₄]²⁻ (paramagnetic), [Zn(NH₃)₄]²⁺ (diamagnetic), [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ (diamagnetic), [PdCl₄]²⁻ (diamagnetic). Thus total paramagnetic sp³ = 2.",
